# docker/MINER_IMAGE -- the prebuilt miner image deploy/join.sh may pull instead of building one.
#
# READ FROM THE CLONE, NEVER FROM network-info.txt. network-info is fetched over plain HTTP, so an
# image named there would be chosen by whoever sits on the path, and join.sh runs that image next to
# the volume that holds the miner's keys. This file arrives with the repository, over HTTPS git, like
# docker/GENESIS_SHA256. join.sh ignores a DENDRA_MINER_IMAGE served by network-info.
#
# HOW IT IS WRITTEN. After a release, take the two lines of that release's miner-image.txt asset
# (.github/workflows/release.yml, job miner-image), put them below these comments, and commit them:
#   # built-for: kit_version=<K> consensus_epoch=<E> release=<vX.Y.Z>
#   DENDRA_MINER_IMAGE=ghcr.io/dendranetwork/dendra-miner@sha256:<64 lowercase hex>
# join.sh accepts exactly that value form -- the official namespace and a full digest, never a tag -- and
# builds the image from the clone otherwise, saying why. It starts a pulled image only when its platform
# is that of the Docker engine: a digest names the bytes of one platform, and the release builds the image
# on its own runner. The package has to be public: when the pull
# fails, join.sh warns and builds from the clone as well.
#
# THE PIN HOLDS ONLY FOR THE GATES IT WAS BUILT FOR. The `built-for` line names them. The repository is
# not published while they differ from docker/KIT_VERSION or docker/CONSENSUS_EPOCH, and a release is not
# tagged while a value is present: the image would run code another kit was released with. Empty the
# value line, with its `built-for` line, before either moves; the next release pins its own image.
#
# With no value line below, join.sh builds the miner image from the clone.
